        "narration": "Sam is getting ready for soccer. He has one water jug. The jug is one whole. Sam splits it into 10 equal parts. Each part is one tenth.",
        "text": "1 whole = 10 tenths",
        "visual": { "kind": "ten-frame", "emoji": null, "count": null, "filled": 10 }
      },
      {
        "narration": "Sam fills 6 of the 10 parts. That is 6 tenths. As a decimal, it looks like 0.6.",
        "text": "6 tenths = 0.6",
        "visual": { "kind": "ten-frame", "emoji": null, "count": null, "filled": 6 }
      },
      {
        "narration": "The dot is called the decimal point. The 0 before it means no whole jugs. The first digit after the dot counts tenths.",
        "text": "0 . 6",
        "visual": null
      },
      {
        "narration": "Now Sam fills 1 whole jug and 4 more tenths. That is 1 and 4 tenths. It looks like 1.4. The 1 counts wholes. The 4 counts tenths.",
        "text": "1.4",
        "visual": { "kind": "ten-frame", "emoji": null, "count": null, "filled": 4 }
      },
      {
        "narration": "To compare, look at the wholes first. 3.2 is less than 5.1, because 3 is less than 5.",
        "text": "3.2 is less than 5.1",
        "visual": null
      },
      {
        "narration": "If the wholes match, compare the tenths. 24.7 is greater than 24.3, because 7 tenths is more than 3 tenths.",
        "text": "24.7 is greater than 24.3",
        "visual": null
      },
      {
        "narration": "A zero in the tenths place means no tenths. So 5.0 is the same as 5. Now let's practise with Sam!",
        "text": "5.0 = 5",
        "visual": null
      }
    ],
